$ MacNeal & Harder (1985), Sec. 8 - pinched cylinder. REFINED MESH.
$
$ Same problem as pinched_cylinder.bdf -- R = 300, L = 600, t = 3, E = 3.0e6,
$ nu = 0.3, rigid end diaphragms, two opposing radial point loads P = 1 at
$ midspan, octant model -- on a 24 x 24 mesh instead of the published 4 x 4 one.
$
$ WHY THIS DECK EXISTS. The pinched cylinder is the slowest-converging case in
$ the set: on the standard 4x4 octant mesh NO published 4-node shell element
$ exceeds 0.64 of the target and most sit near 0.39, with OpenJFEM at 0.52 and
$ the reference solver at 0.52. Refining is the only way to separate the
$ element from the mesh. This deck is the convergence evidence;
$ pinched_cylinder.bdf remains the benchmark.
$
$ MESH RULE (so this deck is reproducible without a generator):
$   node id = 25*j + i + 1,  i in 0..24 (z), j in 0..24 (phi)
$   z_i     = i * 300 / 24
$   phi_j   = j * 90 / 24  degrees
$   x = 300 cos(phi), y = 300 sin(phi), z = z_i
$   element (i,j) = [nid(i,j), nid(i+1,j), nid(i+1,j+1), nid(i,j+1)]
$
$ Applied load at node 1 = P/4 = 0.25 in -X: the load point lies on TWO of the
$ three symmetry planes (z = 0 and phi = 0), so the octant carries a quarter.
$ Reference radial displacement at node 1: |Ux| = 1.8248e-5.
$
SOL 101
CEND
TITLE = MacNeal-Harder pinched cylinder (octant, 24x24)
SUBCASE 1
  LABEL = Two opposing radial point loads, octant model
  SPC = 1
  LOAD = 1
  DISPLACEMENT = ALL
BEGIN BULK
$ ---- solver parameters ----
$ Drilling-DOF stabilisation is not part of the benchmark definition and
$ solvers default it differently (MSC/Nastran 70.5 SOL 101: 0.0; MSC.Nastran
$ 2004+ and OpenJFEM: 100.0). Pinned so the accuracy and same-deck parity
$ comparisons are parameter-exact. See validation/README.md, "PARAM,K6ROT".
PARAM,K6ROT,0.
MAT1, 1, 3.0+6, , 0.3
PSHELL, 1, 1, 3.0, 1
$ ---- grids ----
GRID,     1, ,  300.00000000,    0.00000000,    0.000000
GRID,     2, ,  300.00000000,    0.00000000,   12.500000
